WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.400
All right.

00:00.400 --> 00:04.480
So now at this phase of the projects we usually define the naming conventions.

00:04.480 --> 00:05.360
So what is that.

00:05.400 --> 00:11.960
It is a set of rules that you define for naming everything in the projects, whether it is a database

00:12.000 --> 00:16.080
schema, tables, stored procedures, folders, anything.

00:16.120 --> 00:20.920
And if you don't do that at the early phase of the projects, I promise you chaos can happen.

00:20.920 --> 00:22.000
Because what's going to happen?

00:22.000 --> 00:26.880
You will have different developers in your projects, and each of those developers have their own style,

00:26.880 --> 00:27.400
of course.

00:27.400 --> 00:33.360
So one developer might name a table dimension customers, where everything is lowercase and between

00:33.360 --> 00:34.280
them underscore.

00:34.280 --> 00:39.840
And you have another developer creating another table called dimension Products, but using the camel

00:39.840 --> 00:40.280
case.

00:40.280 --> 00:44.640
So there is no separation between the words and the first character is capitalized, and maybe another

00:44.640 --> 00:48.720
one using some prefixes like dim underscore categories.

00:48.720 --> 00:50.880
So we have here like a shortcut of the dimension.

00:50.880 --> 00:53.840
So as you can see there are different designs and styles.

00:53.840 --> 00:57.160
And if we leave the door open, what can happen in the middle of the projects.

00:57.160 --> 01:03.560
You will notice, okay, everything looks inconsistent and you can define a big task to go and rename

01:03.560 --> 01:05.570
everything following a specific role.

01:05.570 --> 01:11.570
So instead of wasting all this time at this phase, you go and define the naming conventions and let's

01:11.570 --> 01:12.450
go and do that.

01:12.450 --> 01:15.370
So we will start with a very important decision.

01:15.370 --> 01:19.290
And that is which naming convention we're going to follow in the whole project.

01:19.290 --> 01:26.370
So you have different cases like the camel case, the Pascal case, the kebab case and the snake case.

01:26.370 --> 01:31.490
And for this project we're going to go with the snake case, where all the letters of a word are going

01:31.490 --> 01:33.170
to be lowercase.

01:33.170 --> 01:36.690
And the separation between words can be an underscore.

01:36.730 --> 01:42.530
For example, a table name called customer info, customer is lowercase, info is as well lowercase

01:42.530 --> 01:44.370
and between them an underscore.

01:44.370 --> 01:48.370
So this is always the first thing that you have to decide for your data projects.

01:48.370 --> 01:50.970
The second thing is to decide the language.

01:50.970 --> 01:55.850
So for example I work in Germany and there is always like a decision that we have to make whether we

01:55.850 --> 01:57.370
use Germany or English.

01:57.370 --> 02:00.930
So we have to decide for our project which language we're going to use.

02:00.970 --> 02:05.530
And a very important general rule that avoid reserved words.

02:05.530 --> 02:11.900
So don't use SQL reserved word as an object name like for example table don't give a table name as a

02:11.900 --> 02:12.340
table.

02:12.340 --> 02:14.500
So those are the general principles.

02:14.500 --> 02:18.660
So those are the general rules that you have to follow in the whole project.

02:18.660 --> 02:23.980
This applies for everything for tables, columns, stored procedures, any names that you are giving

02:23.980 --> 02:24.820
in your scripts.

02:24.860 --> 02:25.540
Now moving on.

02:25.540 --> 02:28.220
We have specifications for the table names.

02:28.220 --> 02:31.380
And here we have different set of rules for each layer.

02:31.380 --> 02:35.100
So here the rule says source system underscore entity.

02:35.100 --> 02:40.540
So we are saying all the tables in the bronze layer should start first with the source system name like

02:40.540 --> 02:42.820
for example CRM or IRB.

02:42.820 --> 02:44.660
And after that we have an underscore.

02:44.700 --> 02:48.180
And then at the end we have the entity name or the table name.

02:48.180 --> 02:51.660
So for example we have this table name CRM underscore.

02:51.780 --> 02:55.580
So that means this table comes from the source system CRM.

02:55.580 --> 02:59.220
And then we have the table name the entity name customer info.

02:59.220 --> 03:03.260
So this is the rule that we're going to follow in naming all tables in the bronze layer.

03:03.300 --> 03:08.820
Then moving on to the silver layer, it is exactly like the bronze because we are not going to rename

03:08.820 --> 03:09.260
anything.

03:09.260 --> 03:12.100
We are not going to build any new data model.

03:12.100 --> 03:15.440
So the naming can be 1 to 1 like the bronze.

03:15.440 --> 03:18.000
So it is exactly the same rules as the bronze.

03:18.000 --> 03:23.920
But if we go to the gold here, since we are building a new data model, we have to go and rename things.

03:23.960 --> 03:29.720
And since as well we are integrating multiple sources together, we will not be using the source system

03:29.720 --> 03:34.000
name in the tables because inside one table you could have multiple sources.

03:34.000 --> 03:39.840
So the rule says all the names must be meaningful business aligned names for the tables, starting with

03:39.840 --> 03:41.280
the category prefix.

03:41.280 --> 03:46.320
So here the rule says it starts with category, then underscore and then entity.

03:46.360 --> 03:50.760
Now what is category we have in the gold layer different types of tables.

03:50.760 --> 03:53.440
So we could build a table called a fact table.

03:53.440 --> 03:55.320
Another one could be a dimension.

03:55.400 --> 03:58.560
A third type could be an aggregation or reports.

03:58.600 --> 04:00.720
So we have different types of tables.

04:00.880 --> 04:04.400
And we can specify those types as a prefix at the start.

04:04.400 --> 04:08.840
So for example we are saying here a facts underscore sales.

04:08.840 --> 04:12.320
So the category is the fact and the table name called sales.

04:12.320 --> 04:15.800
And here I just made like a table with different type of patterns.

04:15.800 --> 04:17.370
So we could have a dimension.

04:17.370 --> 04:20.010
So we say it starts with the dim underscore.

04:20.050 --> 04:23.050
For example dim customers or products.

04:23.050 --> 04:25.330
And then we have another type called fact table.

04:25.330 --> 04:30.850
So it starts with fact underscore or aggregated table where we have the first three characters like

04:30.890 --> 04:33.730
aggregating the customers or the sales monthly.

04:33.730 --> 04:38.770
So as you can see, as you are creating a naming convention, you have first to make it clear what is

04:38.770 --> 04:43.170
the rule, describe each part of the rule and start giving examples.

04:43.170 --> 04:47.290
So with that, we make it clear for the whole team which names they should follow.

04:47.290 --> 04:50.250
So we talked here about the table naming convention.

04:50.250 --> 04:53.410
Then you can as well go and make a naming convention for the columns.

04:53.450 --> 04:58.090
Like for example in the gold layer we're going to go and have surrogate keys so we can define it like

04:58.090 --> 04:58.650
this.

04:58.690 --> 05:02.770
The surrogate key should start with a table name and then underscore a key.

05:02.810 --> 05:05.530
Like for example we can call it customer underscore key.

05:05.570 --> 05:08.010
It is a surrogate key in that dimension.

05:08.010 --> 05:10.570
Customers the same thing for technical columns.

05:10.610 --> 05:16.170
As a data engineer, we might add our own columns to the tables that don't come from the source system.

05:16.170 --> 05:20.650
And those columns are the technical columns, or sometimes we call them metadata columns.

05:20.690 --> 05:27.100
Now in order to separate them from the original columns that comes from the source system, we can have

05:27.100 --> 05:28.500
like a prefix for that.

05:28.500 --> 05:33.180
Like for example, the rule says if you are building any technical or metadata columns, the columns

05:33.180 --> 05:37.380
should start with DW, underscore and then the column name.

05:37.380 --> 05:43.060
For example, if you want the metadata load date, we can have DW underscore load dates.

05:43.100 --> 05:49.340
So with that, if anyone sees that column starts with DW, we understand this data comes from a data

05:49.340 --> 05:49.820
engineer.

05:49.820 --> 05:53.580
And we can keep adding rules like for example, the stored procedure over here.

05:53.580 --> 05:59.100
If you are making an ETL script, then it should start with the prefix load underscore and then the

05:59.100 --> 05:59.420
layer.

05:59.420 --> 06:05.300
For example, the stored procedure that is responsible for loading the bronze can be called load underscore

06:05.300 --> 06:05.860
bronze.

06:05.860 --> 06:08.540
And for the silver load underscore silver.

06:08.540 --> 06:11.660
So those are currently the rules for the stored procedure.

06:11.660 --> 06:13.660
So this is how I do it usually in my projects.

06:13.660 --> 06:14.420
All right my friends.

06:14.420 --> 06:17.780
So with that we have a solid naming conventions for our projects.

06:17.780 --> 06:18.820
So this is done.

06:18.820 --> 06:20.860
And now the next tip that we're going to go to git.

06:20.860 --> 06:23.540
And you will create a brand new repository.

06:23.540 --> 06:25.340
And we're going to prepare its structure.

06:25.340 --> 06:25.980
So let's go.
